  "worked_solution": "First, let's calculate ${x}^{2}\\cdot p(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot (-3{x}^{5})=-3{x}^{7}\n$$\n\nWhen we multiply the two functions ${x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)$ and $q(x)$, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)\\cdot q(x)) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-3{x}^{7}\\cdot \\frac{4}{{x}^{7}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-12 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$",
